{
  "slug": "nhce-python",
  "title": "NHCE - Problem Solving Using Python",
  "description": "Tactical Course for New Horizon College of Engineering (25CSE144)",
  "courseDescription": "Python is one of the most popular programming languages, powering AI, automation, web applications, data science, and more. This course takes you from your very first line of Python code to writing clean, efficient, and reusable programs. Learn core programming concepts, functions, data structures, object-oriented programming, file handling, and exception handling through practical examples, relatable analogies, and hands-on exercises. By the end of the course, you'll have the confidence to build real-world applications and think like a Python programmer.",
  "courseFaqs": [
    {
      "question": "Do I need any prior programming experience to start this course?",
      "answer": "No. This course begins from the absolute basics, covering what programming is and how to set up Python on your computer. If you've never written code before, you'll feel right at home."
    },
    {
      "question": "How is this course different from just reading Python documentation?",
      "answer": "Documentation explains the syntax. This course explains the concepts. Every topic is introduced using real-world analogies, practical examples, and hands-on exercises so you understand why things work, not just how to write them."
    },
    {
      "question": "Will this course help me crack campus placement interviews?",
      "answer": "Yes. The course is designed with placement preparation in mind, covering variables, functions, data structures, object-oriented programming, file handling, exception handling, and problem-solving patterns commonly asked in coding rounds and technical interviews."
    },
    {
      "question": "How long will it take to complete the course?",
      "answer": "It depends on your learning pace, but studying two to three hours a day is enough to comfortably complete the course in four to six weeks. Each unit builds on the previous one, making steady progress more valuable than rushing."
    },
    {
      "question": "I already know basic Python. Should I still start from Unit 1?",
      "answer": "If you're already comfortable with Python fundamentals, you can move ahead to the advanced units. However, the early chapters include practical insights, clean coding practices, and problem-solving techniques that even experienced learners often overlook. A quick review is highly recommended."
    }
  ],
